    "category": "Hematology: Splenic MZL HCV",
    "questionText": "A 60-year-old man with chronic hepatitis C infection (genotype 1) presents with fatigue and abdominal fullness. Vitals: BP 130/78, HR 80. Exam: massive splenomegaly (spleen tip 8 cm below costal margin), no significant peripheral lymphadenopathy. Labs: Hgb 10.5, WBC 8.0 (mild lymphocytosis with 'villous lymphocytes' on smear), platelets 110K, mild transaminitis (AST 65, ALT 78), HCV RNA detectable. Bone marrow biopsy: intrasinusoidal lymphoid infiltrate of small B cells with villous projections. Flow cytometry: CD19+ CD20+ CD5-NEGATIVE CD10-NEGATIVE CD23-NEGATIVE. Cytogenetics: deletion 7q. Which is the most appropriate first-line therapy?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Splenectomy as the only effective therapy", "explanation": "Incorrect. While splenectomy IS a recognized therapy for SMZL (often produces durable responses), it is no longer the obligatory first-line. In HCV-positive SMZL, antiviral therapy of HCV alone can produce lymphoma regression. This would be the correct answer in HCV-negative SMZL with massive symptomatic splenomegaly and no other treatment options." },
      { "text": "Direct-acting antiviral therapy for HCV", "explanation": "Correct. DAA therapy for HCV (e.g., sofosbuvir + velpatasvir, glecaprevir + pibrentasvir) — HCV-driven splenic marginal zone lymphoma can REGRESS with HCV eradication; reassess lymphoma response after sustained virologic response (SVR); add rituximab-based therapy for non-responders or progressive disease. SPLENIC MARGINAL ZONE LYMPHOMA (SMZL) is an indolent B-cell NHL of the splenic marginal zone — characterized by: (1) massive splenomegaly OFTEN WITHOUT significant peripheral lymphadenopathy (key distinction from other NHL); (2) 'VILLOUS LYMPHOCYTES' on peripheral smear (small lymphocytes with short hairy/villous cytoplasmic projections); (3) intrasinusoidal pattern in bone marrow; (4) immunophenotype CD19+ CD20+ CD5-NEGATIVE CD10-NEGATIVE CD23-NEGATIVE (distinguishing from CLL/MCL which are CD5+, and follicular which is CD10+); (5) common 7q deletion; (6) strong association with HEPATITIS C VIRUS infection. KEY UNIQUE FEATURE: HCV-ASSOCIATED SMZL can REGRESS with HCV eradication using direct-acting antivirals (DAAs) — clonal B-cells driven by chronic HCV antigen stimulation can involute when the antigen drive is removed. This is similar to gastric MALT responding to H. pylori eradication. Management approach: (1) HCV-POSITIVE: DAA therapy first; lymphoma often regresses with SVR; reassess at 6-12 months; (2) HCV-NEGATIVE OR persistent lymphoma despite SVR: depends on symptoms — watchful waiting if asymptomatic; symptomatic disease → RITUXIMAB monotherapy (highly effective in SMZL), or rituximab + chemo (BR), or SPLENECTOMY (especially for symptomatic massive splenomegaly without disseminated disease — historically standard, now competing with rituximab); (3) For relapsed/refractory: BTK inhibitors, lenalidomide + rituximab, allogeneic SCT in select cases. HEPATITIS C ASSOCIATIONS to recall: cryoglobulinemia (mixed type II), HCC, splenic marginal zone lymphoma, glomerulonephritis (membranoproliferative)." },
      { "text": "R-CHOP for 6 cycles immediately for all NHL", "explanation": "Incorrect. R-CHOP is appropriate for AGGRESSIVE NHL like DLBCL; for indolent SMZL, less aggressive approaches (HCV treatment, watchful waiting, rituximab monotherapy) are preferred. This would be the correct answer in DLBCL." },
      { "text": "Allogeneic SCT as first-line", "explanation": "Incorrect. Indolent SMZL has a long natural history; SCT is reserved for transformation to aggressive lymphoma or multiply refractory disease. This would be the correct answer in transformed/aggressive disease in a young patient." },
      { "text": "Hydroxyurea single-agent", "explanation": "Incorrect. Hydroxyurea is not used in SMZL or other lymphomas. It is used in MPNs and sickle cell disease. This would be the correct answer in those diseases." }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: Splenic MZL = massive splenomegaly + VILLOUS LYMPHOCYTES + CD19+CD20+CD5-CD10-CD23-. STRONG HCV association. HCV-positive SMZL → treat HCV with DAAs first; lymphoma often regresses. Persistent → rituximab ± splenectomy.",
    "pdfPage": 5,
    "pdfQuote": "marginal zone"
