    "id": 11,
    "category": "Hematology: Hodgkin Late Effects",
    "questionText": "A 45-year-old woman has been in remission from nodular sclerosing Hodgkin lymphoma for 18 years after ABVD + mediastinal radiation at age 27. She comes for follow-up. Which is the most important LATE EFFECT screening she should receive based on her prior treatment exposure?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Annual colonoscopy starting now for colorectal cancer (radiation-related)", "explanation": "Incorrect. Mediastinal/upper-body radiation does not significantly increase colorectal cancer risk. Standard age-based colorectal cancer screening at age 45-50 applies. This would be the correct answer after pelvic radiation (which can increase rectal cancer risk)." },
      { "text": "Annual breast MRI + mammography starting 8-10 yrs post-radiation", "explanation": "Correct. Annual breast cancer screening with BREAST MRI (in addition to mammography) starting 8-10 years after thoracic radiation OR at age 30, due to high lifetime risk of secondary breast cancer (~20-30% by age 50 in women irradiated before age 30); also screen for cardiovascular disease, thyroid dysfunction, second malignancies (lung, sarcoma). WOMEN IRRADIATED TO THE CHEST FOR HODGKIN LYMPHOMA BEFORE AGE 30 have a markedly elevated risk of SECONDARY BREAST CANCER (~20-30% lifetime risk by age 50, similar to BRCA mutation carriers). Screening recommendations: ANNUAL BREAST MRI + MAMMOGRAPHY starting 8-10 years after radiation OR at age 30 (whichever comes first), with MRI in alternating intervals with mammography (e.g., breast MRI in one 6-month interval, mammogram in the other for high-risk surveillance). OTHER MAJOR LATE EFFECTS of HL treatment to screen: (1) SECONDARY MALIGNANCIES — breast (women, after thoracic radiation), lung (radiation + smoking synergy), thyroid (after neck radiation), sarcoma (in radiation field), AML/MDS (from alkylators in BEACOPP), Hodgkin/NHL itself can recur; (2) CARDIOVASCULAR DISEASE — premature CAD (from radiation + anthracycline cardiotoxicity), valvular disease (especially after mediastinal radiation), cardiomyopathy (anthracycline cumulative dose-related), pericardial disease, conduction abnormalities. Recommend cardiovascular risk factor management, periodic echocardiogram, stress testing as appropriate. (3) THYROID DYSFUNCTION (hypothyroidism most common, sometimes Graves disease or thyroid cancer) — annual TSH after neck/mediastinal radiation. (4) PULMONARY DISEASE — bleomycin lung toxicity (interstitial fibrosis), radiation pneumonitis. PFTs as indicated. (5) INFERTILITY/EARLY MENOPAUSE (alkylators, pelvic radiation, BEACOPP). (6) SECONDARY MYELODYSPLASIA/AML (from alkylators, especially BEACOPP). Mediastinal radiation is being de-escalated in modern protocols (lower doses, smaller fields, response-adapted to PET) — but historical exposures cause these late effects. Education of HL survivors about late effects is essential." },
      { "text": "Annual whole-body bone scan for skeletal metastases", "explanation": "Incorrect. There is no role for routine whole-body bone scanning in HL survivors without symptoms — HL survivorship surveillance focuses on secondary malignancies (especially breast in young women), cardiovascular disease, and thyroid dysfunction. This would be the correct answer in prostate or breast cancer surveillance with bone-targeting tumors." },
      { "text": "Routine annual brain MRI for primary CNS lymphoma", "explanation": "Incorrect. HL does not typically metastasize to the CNS, and prior HL does not particularly elevate primary CNS lymphoma risk. PCNSL is associated with HIV and immunodeficiency. This would be the correct answer in HIV with low CD4 with concerning neurologic symptoms." },
      { "text": "Annual upper endoscopy for esophageal cancer", "explanation": "Incorrect. Esophageal cancer risk is mildly elevated after mediastinal radiation but is not a primary screening recommendation in HL survivors. Breast cancer surveillance, cardiovascular screening, and thyroid surveillance are higher priority. This would be the correct answer in Barrett's esophagus surveillance." }
    ],
