    "id": 14,
    "category": "Hematology: Primary Mediastinal Large B-Cell",
    "questionText": "A 28-year-old woman presents with a 4-week history of progressive dyspnea, dry cough, facial swelling worse in the morning, and dilated neck veins (SVC syndrome features). Vitals: T 37.1°C, HR 96, BP 110/68 (with pulsus paradoxus), RR 22, O2 sat 94%. Exam: facial plethora, dilated neck veins, decreased breath sounds at bases. CT chest: 12 cm bulky anterior mediastinal mass with SVC compression and bilateral pleural effusions. Biopsy of the mass: sheets of large pleomorphic CD19+ CD20+ CD30 variably positive B cells with associated fibrosis. FISH: NO t(15;17), NO t(8;14), NO MYC/BCL-2 rearrangements. Immunophenotype consistent with PRIMARY MEDIASTINAL LARGE B-CELL LYMPHOMA (PMLBCL). Which is the most appropriate first-line therapy?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Standard ABVD + radiation as for classical Hodgkin lymphoma", "explanation": "Incorrect. ABVD is for classical Hodgkin lymphoma. PMLBCL is a distinct DLBCL subtype with biology overlapping HL (both arise from thymic B cells, both have PD-L1 amplification, both can present with mediastinal bulky disease) but is a B-cell NHL requiring B-cell-directed therapy. This would be the correct answer in classical HL." },
      { "text": "DA-EPOCH-R for 6 cycles (preferred over R-CHOP for PMLBCL)", "explanation": "Correct. DA-EPOCH-R (dose-adjusted etoposide, prednisone, vincristine, cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, rituximab) is generally preferred over R-CHOP for PMLBCL based on intensive 6-cycle dose-adjusted protocol; achieves high cure rates without obligatory radiation; alternatively R-CHOP + radiation; brentuximab and immune checkpoint inhibitors (pembrolizumab, nivolumab) for relapsed disease. PRIMARY MEDIASTINAL LARGE B-CELL LYMPHOMA (PMLBCL) is a distinct subtype of DLBCL with: (1) demographics — predominantly YOUNG WOMEN (median age ~35); (2) presentation — BULKY ANTERIOR MEDIASTINAL MASS (arising from thymic B cells), often with SVC syndrome, dyspnea, cough, pleural/pericardial effusions; (3) histology — DLBCL morphology with diffuse fibrosis; (4) immunophenotype — CD19+ CD20+ CD30 variably positive (intermediate between DLBCL and classical HL); (5) molecular — amplification of 9p24.1 (PD-L1/PD-L2, like classical HL) → highly responsive to PD-1 inhibitors; (6) GENERALLY GOOD PROGNOSIS with appropriate first-line therapy. FIRST-LINE THERAPY: (1) DA-EPOCH-R (dose-adjusted etoposide + prednisone + vincristine + cyclophosphamide + doxorubicin + rituximab) for 6 cycles — preferred by many centers based on excellent outcomes in NCI prospective study (>90% cure rates without radiation in many patients); the 'dose-adjusted' part means escalating doses based on hematologic tolerance; advantage: avoids mediastinal radiation in young women (reduces secondary breast cancer and cardiovascular late effects); (2) Alternative: R-CHOP + mediastinal radiation therapy — also effective but with radiation-related late effects; (3) END-OF-TREATMENT PET-CT to assess response; (4) RESIDUAL FDG-AVIDITY after chemotherapy can be PET-AVID FROM INFLAMMATION/FIBROSIS (PMLBCL has particularly high rate of false-positive PET due to inflammatory background) — biopsy or close follow-up rather than reflexive radiation. RELAPSED/REFRACTORY: (1) Salvage chemotherapy (R-DHAP, R-ICE, R-GDP) → autologous SCT in chemo-sensitive patients; (2) IMMUNE CHECKPOINT INHIBITORS (PEMBROLIZUMAB FDA-approved for relapsed PMLBCL; nivolumab also active) — exploit PD-L1 amplification; (3) BRENTUXIMAB VEDOTIN (CD30 expression in PMLBCL); (4) CD19 CAR-T therapy (axicabtagene, lisocabtagene); (5) Allogeneic SCT in select patients. PROGNOSIS: with DA-EPOCH-R, >80-90% long-term cure. Differential diagnosis of mediastinal masses in young women: PMLBCL, classical HL (nodular sclerosing especially), thymoma, germ cell tumor, T-ALL/lymphoblastic lymphoma." },
      { "text": "Single-agent rituximab for life", "explanation": "Incorrect. Single-agent rituximab is inadequate for an aggressive lymphoma like PMLBCL. Combination chemoimmunotherapy is needed. This would be the correct answer in low-grade indolent disease maintenance." },
      { "text": "Watchful waiting with serial imaging", "explanation": "Incorrect. PMLBCL is aggressive with rapid progression and life-threatening SVC syndrome — urgent treatment is required. Watchful waiting is appropriate ONLY for indolent low-burden disease without active symptoms. This would be the correct answer NEVER in PMLBCL." },
      { "text": "Allogeneic stem cell transplant in first complete remission", "explanation": "Incorrect. PMLBCL has good outcomes with chemoimmunotherapy alone (DA-EPOCH-R) — allogeneic SCT in first CR is not standard. SCT is reserved for relapsed/refractory disease after autologous SCT failure. This would be the correct answer in refractory PMLBCL after multiple lines." }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: PMLBCL = young woman + bulky anterior mediastinal mass + SVC syndrome + DLBCL morphology + PD-L1 amplification. First-line = DA-EPOCH-R (NCI protocol, avoids radiation). Relapsed: salvage chemo → auto-SCT; pembrolizumab/nivolumab (PD-L1 amplification); brentuximab; CD19 CAR-T.",
    "pdfPage": 5,
    "pdfQuote": "DLBCL"
