    "id": 15,
    "category": "Hematology: Double-Hit Lymphoma",
    "questionText": "A 56-year-old man presents with rapidly enlarging cervical and abdominal lymphadenopathy, B symptoms, and weight loss over 6 weeks. Vitals: BP 130/78, HR 90, T 38.0°C. Exam: bulky cervical and inguinal nodes, mild hepatomegaly. Labs: WBC 18 with circulating large cells, Hgb 11, platelets 200K, LDH 2,800 (markedly elevated). Excisional cervical node biopsy: sheets of large CD19+ CD20+ B cells with high mitotic activity, Ki-67 90%. FISH: BOTH c-MYC translocation AND BCL-2 translocation POSITIVE ('DOUBLE-HIT' lymphoma; some centers also call this 'high-grade B-cell lymphoma with MYC and BCL2 rearrangements'). Which is the most appropriate first-line therapy?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Standard R-CHOP for 6 cycles", "explanation": "Incorrect. DOUBLE-HIT LYMPHOMAS (MYC + BCL-2 rearrangements, sometimes with BCL-6 making it 'triple-hit') have a notably WORSE PROGNOSIS with standard R-CHOP than typical DLBCL. More intensive regimens improve outcomes. This would be the correct answer in standard DLBCL without MYC/BCL-2 rearrangements." },
      { "text": "DA-EPOCH-R with intrathecal CNS prophylaxis", "explanation": "Correct. Dose-intensive regimen: DA-EPOCH-R (dose-adjusted etoposide + prednisone + vincristine + cyclophosphamide + doxorubicin + rituximab) for 6 cycles OR R-HyperCVAD-like regimen, with INTRATHECAL CNS PROPHYLAXIS; consider autologous SCT in first CR for selected patients; CAR-T or bispecific antibodies for relapsed disease. DOUBLE-HIT LYMPHOMA (DHL, now classified as 'high-grade B-cell lymphoma with MYC and BCL2 rearrangements,' HGBCL-DH) has CONCURRENT REARRANGEMENTS of c-MYC (chromosome 8) AND BCL-2 (chromosome 18), with or without BCL-6 rearrangement ('triple-hit'). Pathogenesis: c-MYC drives proliferation; BCL-2 prevents apoptosis — the combination produces a particularly aggressive lymphoma with biological features overlapping Burkitt (high proliferation) and follicular (BCL-2 overexpression). FEATURES: AGGRESSIVE clinical course, frequently with B symptoms, bulky disease, elevated LDH, bone marrow and CNS involvement. PROGNOSIS with standard R-CHOP is POOR (40-50% long-term survival), prompting use of intensified regimens. FIRST-LINE THERAPY: (1) DA-EPOCH-R for 6 cycles — preferred by many based on retrospective data showing improved outcomes vs R-CHOP; the dose-adjusted protocol intensifies treatment based on hematologic tolerance; (2) Alternative intensive regimens: R-HyperCVAD/MA (alternating high-dose chemo with methotrexate and cytarabine), R-CODOX-M/IVAC (Magrath); (3) INTRATHECAL CNS PROPHYLAXIS (intrathecal MTX ± cytarabine) is critical — DHL has high CNS relapse risk (>10%); some regimens include high-dose systemic MTX; (4) AUTOLOGOUS SCT in first CR — controversial in this era; some advocate for fit patients with high-risk features; ongoing study; (5) Pretreatment WORKUP: lumbar puncture (CNS staging), echocardiogram (anthracycline), TLS prophylaxis (hydration + allopurinol or rasburicase given high LDH). RELAPSED/REFRACTORY DHL: very poor prognosis; CD19 CAR-T (axicabtagene, lisocabtagene, tisagenlecleucel) — FDA-approved for relapsed DLBCL/HGBCL after 2 prior lines (or 1 prior line for aggressive disease per ZUMA-7/TRANSFORM/BELINDA); BISPECIFIC ANTIBODIES (glofitamab, epcoritamab — CD20×CD3); polatuzumab vedotin (anti-CD79b ADC); allogeneic SCT in selected cases. Important to differentiate from MYC-rearrangement-only (single-hit MYC+, prognosis similar to DLBCL) and double-EXPRESSOR (immunohistochemistry coexpression of MYC and BCL-2 protein, without translocations — intermediate prognosis, treated as DLBCL)." },
      { "text": "Watchful waiting since the patient has stable disease", "explanation": "Incorrect. DHL is aggressive and rapidly progressive; observation is fatal within weeks. This would be the correct answer in indolent low-grade disease (e.g., asymptomatic follicular)." },
      { "text": "ATRA + arsenic trioxide as for APL", "explanation": "Incorrect. ATRA + ATO is for APL (M3 AML with t(15;17)) — not DHL. This would be the correct answer in APL." },
      { "text": "Single-agent rituximab indefinitely as maintenance", "explanation": "Incorrect. Single-agent rituximab is inadequate for aggressive lymphoma. Combination intensive chemotherapy is required. This would be the correct answer for low-grade indolent maintenance." }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: Double-hit lymphoma (MYC + BCL-2 ± BCL-6 rearrangements) = AGGRESSIVE, POOR prognosis with R-CHOP. Use DA-EPOCH-R or R-HyperCVAD + INTRATHECAL CNS prophylaxis. Relapse: CD19 CAR-T, bispecifics, polatuzumab.",
    "pdfPage": 5,
    "pdfQuote": "diffuse large B-cell"
