    "id": 16,
    "category": "Hematology: Hairy Cell Variant Recognition",
    "questionText": "A 58-year-old man presents with progressive fatigue, fevers, and abdominal fullness. Vitals: BP 130/78, HR 80. Exam: massive splenomegaly. Labs: WBC 32,000 (mostly atypical lymphocytes with cytoplasmic projections), Hgb 9.5, platelets 70K. Bone marrow biopsy: interstitial infiltrate of small lymphoid cells with cytoplasmic projections. Flow cytometry: CD20+ CD11c+ CD103+ but CD25-NEGATIVE (unusual) and CD123-NEGATIVE; BRAF V600E mutation NEGATIVE; MAP2K1 mutation POSITIVE. Which is the most likely diagnosis and best management approach?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Classic hairy cell leukemia — treat with cladribine alone for durable remission", "explanation": "Incorrect. Classic hairy cell leukemia is characterized by CD25+ CD123+ BRAF V600E POSITIVE phenotype. This patient is CD25-NEGATIVE, CD123-negative, BRAF V600E-NEGATIVE but has MAP2K1 mutation — a different entity. While cladribine is first-line for classic hairy cell, the variant (HCL-v) responds less well. This would be the correct answer in classic HCL with BRAF V600E and CD25+." },
      { "text": "Hairy cell variant (HCL-v); purine analog + rituximab", "explanation": "Correct. HCL-v is a distinct entity from classic HCL, BRAF-WILD-TYPE, CD25-NEGATIVE, often MAP2K1 mutated; LESS RESPONSIVE to purine analogs alone; treat with PURINE ANALOG (cladribine or pentostatin) + RITUXIMAB combination; consider venetoclax, BRAF/MEK inhibitors based on mutation, or moxetumomab for relapsed disease. HAIRY CELL LEUKEMIA VARIANT (HCL-v) is a distinct entity from classic HCL, accounting for ~10% of cases that look like HCL clinically. KEY DIFFERENCES from classic HCL: (1) IMMUNOPHENOTYPE: HCL-v is CD25-NEGATIVE, CD123-NEGATIVE, often FMC7+ (vs classic HCL CD25+ CD123+); (2) MOLECULAR: HCL-v is BRAF V600E-NEGATIVE (vs >90% positive in classic HCL); MAP2K1 (MEK1) mutations and TP53 mutations are common in HCL-v; (3) CLINICAL: HCL-v often presents with HIGHER WBC (leukocytosis vs cytopenia in classic), more aggressive course, often elderly patients; (4) TREATMENT RESPONSE: HCL-v RESPONDS LESS WELL to single-agent purine analogs (cladribine, pentostatin) than classic HCL — only ~50% CR with cladribine alone vs ~80% in classic HCL. MANAGEMENT of HCL-v: (1) Combination CLADRIBINE + RITUXIMAB is preferred over cladribine alone — improves CR rates; (2) PENTOSTATIN + RITUXIMAB is alternative; (3) For relapsed/refractory: BENDAMUSTINE + RITUXIMAB; MOXETUMOMAB PASUDOTOX (anti-CD22 immunotoxin, FDA-approved for relapsed HCL); BTK inhibitors (ibrutinib); VENETOCLAX (BCL-2 inhibitor); MEK inhibitors (TRAMETINIB) in MAP2K1-mutant cases (specific targeted therapy based on genotype); allogeneic SCT in young fit patients. Classic HCL with BRAF V600E can also use VEMURAFENIB (BRAF inhibitor) ± rituximab in relapsed disease — targeted to the mutation. The distinction between classic HCL and HCL-v is important because treatment approach and prognosis differ. SPLENECTOMY is no longer first-line for either entity in the modern era. HCL-v has a worse prognosis than classic HCL." },
      { "text": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia — observe with watchful waiting", "explanation": "Incorrect. CLL would show smudge cells and CD5+ CD19+ CD23+ B cells with normal splenomegaly (often less massive). Hairy cell variants are distinct with their characteristic morphology (cytoplasmic projections) and immunophenotype. This would be the correct answer in early-stage CLL." },
      { "text": "Marginal zone lymphoma — treat with rituximab alone", "explanation": "Incorrect. While SMZL can show villous lymphocytes and splenomegaly (similar clinical picture), HCL-v has distinct flow cytometry (CD11c+ CD103+) and bone marrow pattern. This would be the correct answer in SMZL." },
      { "text": "Burkitt lymphoma — DA-EPOCH-R with TLS prophylaxis", "explanation": "Incorrect. Burkitt is a high-grade aggressive lymphoma with starry sky histology and Ki-67 ~100% — not the indolent HCL-variant picture. This would be the correct answer in Burkitt." }
    ],
