    "id": 17,
    "category": "Hematology: Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant HL",
    "questionText": "A 27-year-old man presents with painless, slowly enlarging right cervical lymphadenopathy over 1 year. No B symptoms. Vitals: BP 122/74, HR 78. Exam: 2.5 cm right cervical lymph node, no other adenopathy or organomegaly. Excisional biopsy: nodular pattern, with 'POPCORN CELLS' (L&H cells — large cells with multilobed/popcorn-shaped nuclei) scattered in a background of small B cells and follicular dendritic meshworks. IHC: L&H cells are CD20-POSITIVE, CD45-POSITIVE, CD15-NEGATIVE, CD30-NEGATIVE. PET/CT: localized to single cervical site (stage IA). Which is the most appropriate management?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Standard ABVD for advanced HL even though disease appears localized", "explanation": "Incorrect. NODULAR LYMPHOCYTE-PREDOMINANT HODGKIN LYMPHOMA (NLPHL) is a distinct entity from classical HL with different biology and treatment approach. The popcorn cells (L&H cells) are CD20+ CD45+ CD15-NEGATIVE CD30-NEGATIVE — opposite of classical HL RS cells. For early-stage NLPHL, INVOLVED-SITE RADIATION ALONE is often curative. ABVD over-treats early-stage NLPHL. This would be the correct answer in advanced-stage classical HL." },
      { "text": "Involved-site radiation therapy (ISRT) alone for stage IA NLPHL", "explanation": "Correct. NODULAR LYMPHOCYTE-PREDOMINANT HODGKIN LYMPHOMA (NLPHL) is a separate entity from CLASSICAL Hodgkin lymphoma in the WHO classification. KEY DIFFERENCES: (1) MALIGNANT CELLS — 'POPCORN CELLS' (L&H cells, lymphocyte-predominant cells, sometimes called LP cells) are large cells with multilobed nuclei — DERIVED FROM germinal center B cells; (2) IMMUNOPHENOTYPE — CD20-POSITIVE (key — B-cell marker preserved), CD45-POSITIVE (CLL antigen preserved), CD15-NEGATIVE, CD30-NEGATIVE, often EMA+, OCT2+ — essentially the OPPOSITE of classical HL RS cells; (3) CLINICAL — usually YOUNG MEN with peripheral (cervical, axillary, inguinal) lymphadenopathy; mediastinal involvement uncommon; B symptoms rare; INDOLENT course (often more like follicular lymphoma than HL); (4) PROGNOSIS — excellent overall; long-term cure rates >90% in early stage; (5) Transformation to DLBCL can occur (5-15% over years). MANAGEMENT: (1) EARLY STAGE (stage IA-IIA, single site) — INVOLVED-SITE RADIATION THERAPY (ISRT) alone is the standard, producing >90% long-term disease control with minimal toxicity. Some advocate for limited-extent surgical excision alone in select cases. (2) Alternative for select patients (avoiding radiation): RITUXIMAB monotherapy (the CD20 expression makes NLPHL highly responsive to rituximab); useful in pediatric patients to avoid radiation late effects. (3) ADVANCED STAGE (III-IV) or relapsed NLPHL — treatment more like B-cell NHL: R-CHOP, R-ABVD (rituximab + ABVD), R-CHVPP, or rituximab + bendamustine. Some use ABVD ± rituximab for stage II-III. (4) Watch for TRANSFORMATION to aggressive B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL or T-cell/histiocyte-rich large B-cell lymphoma — THRLBCL). NLPHL is a SEPARATE LYMPHOMA from classical HL in current classifications and should not be conflated." },
      { "text": "ATRA + arsenic trioxide as for APL", "explanation": "Incorrect. ATRA + ATO is for APL — not NLPHL. This would be the correct answer in APL." },
      { "text": "Single-agent venetoclax indefinitely", "explanation": "Incorrect. Venetoclax (BCL-2 inhibitor) is used in CLL and select BCL-2 rearranged lymphomas — not standard for NLPHL. This would be the correct answer in CLL." },
      { "text": "Allogeneic SCT immediately as primary treatment", "explanation": "Incorrect. NLPHL has excellent prognosis with simple ISRT or rituximab; allogeneic SCT is overkill. SCT may be considered for refractory or transformed disease. This would be the correct answer in refractory transformed NLPHL." }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: NLPHL = 'popcorn cells' (CD20+CD45+CD15-CD30-) in young men with peripheral LAD. INDOLENT, B-cell-like. Stage IA = ISRT alone (>90% cure). Advanced = R-based regimens (rituximab + ABVD/CHOP/bendamustine). Watch for transformation to DLBCL.",
