    "id": 18,
    "category": "Hematology: Enteropathy-Associated T-Cell Lymphoma",
    "questionText": "A 58-year-old woman with known celiac disease (diagnosed 20 years ago, intermittently adherent to gluten-free diet) presents with worsening abdominal pain, weight loss, fevers, and new-onset diarrhea over 3 months. Vitals: T 38.0°C, BP 110/68, HR 96. Exam: cachectic, mild diffuse abdominal tenderness. Labs: anemia, mild transaminitis, elevated LDH. CT abdomen: thickening of jejunal wall and several enlarged mesenteric lymph nodes. Capsule endoscopy: multiple ulcerated jejunal lesions. Biopsy: pleomorphic medium-sized T cells with abundant cytoplasm, CD3+ CD8+ CD56+ T-cell receptor gamma-delta or alpha-beta rearrangement; HLA-DQ2 positive. Which is the most likely diagnosis?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Refractory celiac disease type I (responsive to immunosuppression)", "explanation": "Incorrect. Refractory celiac disease type I has aberrant intraepithelial lymphocytes that retain normal phenotype (CD3+ CD8+ TCR+) and can respond to budesonide/immunosuppression. It does not show mass lesions, ulcerated tumor-forming lymphoma morphology, or LDH elevation. This would be the correct answer in refractory celiac without overt lymphoma." },
      { "text": "Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma (EATL) — a complication of long-standing celiac disease", "explanation": "Correct. ENTEROPATHY-ASSOCIATED T-CELL LYMPHOMA (EATL) is a rare aggressive peripheral T-cell lymphoma of the small intestine, occurring almost exclusively in patients with long-standing CELIAC DISEASE (HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8 positive). Classification: EATL type I (now called 'EATL' in current WHO) — strongly associated with celiac; pleomorphic medium-large T cells; CD3+ CD7+ CD8 variable, CD30 often positive, TCR αβ. Monomorphic epitheliotropic intestinal T-cell lymphoma (MEITL, formerly EATL type II) — less strongly associated with celiac; monomorphic small T cells; CD8+ CD56+ TCR γδ. CLINICAL FEATURES: (1) chronic abdominal pain, weight loss, diarrhea; (2) often presents with COMPLICATIONS — bowel obstruction, perforation, ulcers, bleeding; (3) jejunum > ileum; (4) mesenteric lymphadenopathy; (5) B symptoms common; (6) high LDH. DIAGNOSIS: capsule endoscopy + double-balloon enteroscopy + biopsy; surgical resection sometimes needed for definitive diagnosis. MANAGEMENT: (1) STRICT GLUTEN-FREE DIET (important even in established EATL); (2) CHEMOTHERAPY: CHOP-based regimens (or CHOEP) with intent to consolidate with autologous SCT in first CR — outcomes poor; (3) BRENTUXIMAB VEDOTIN + CHP (BV-CHP) for CD30+ EATL based on extrapolation from ECHELON-2; (4) AUTOLOGOUS SCT in first CR is recommended for fit patients; (5) Allogeneic SCT for relapsed/refractory; (6) PROGNOSIS: POOR overall (5-year survival 20-30%). PREVENTION: strict adherence to gluten-free diet in celiac patients markedly reduces EATL risk over decades — emphasizes importance of adherence. Other celiac-associated complications: small bowel ADENOCARCINOMA, REFRACTORY celiac disease (Type I and Type II — Type II has aberrant clonal IELs and high progression to EATL)." },
      { "text": "Crohn disease with deep mucosal involvement", "explanation": "Incorrect. Crohn disease causes transmural inflammation with skip lesions, granulomas, fistulas — not pleomorphic T-cell lymphocyte infiltrates with monoclonal TCR rearrangement and LDH elevation. The biopsy findings here are diagnostic of EATL, not Crohn. This would be the correct answer in Crohn with appropriate histopathology." },
      { "text": "Whipple disease", "explanation": "Incorrect. Whipple disease shows PAS-positive macrophages in lamina propria (foamy macrophages containing Tropheryma whipplei); not a lymphoma. This would be the correct answer with PAS-positive macrophages and arthralgia/CNS findings." },
      { "text": "Tropical sprue", "explanation": "Incorrect. Tropical sprue presents in patients from tropical regions with chronic diarrhea, malabsorption, and folate/B12 deficiency; not a lymphoma. This would be the correct answer in a patient from a tropical region with mucosal villous flattening responding to tetracycline + folate." }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: EATL = aggressive peripheral T-cell lymphoma in long-standing CELIAC patients (HLA-DQ2/DQ8). Jejunal mass + B symptoms + high LDH. Treat: gluten-free diet + CHOP/CHOEP/BV-CHP + auto-SCT in first CR. Poor prognosis. Strict celiac diet adherence reduces risk.",
    "pdfPage": 6,
